<worksheet xml:id="se1b">
  <title>Linear Algebra Sample Exam 1B</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Work done on scratch paper will not be graded. You don't need to show your work on pages 1 and 2.</em>
    </p>
  </introduction>

<!-- Page 1 -->
<page>
  <!-- Question 1 -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (5 points) Indicate whether the situations are <term>possible</term> or <term>impossible</term>.
      </p>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <col width="76%" halign="left" />
          <col width="12%" halign="center" />
          <col width="12%" halign="center" />
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>possible</cell>
            <cell>impossible</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>a) Vector <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^m</m> is in the span of the columns of <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> and the linear system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is inconsistent, where <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n</m>.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>b) <m>V</m> is a collection of three vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. No two vectors in <m>V</m> are scalar multiples of each other, but the vectors in <m>V</m> are linearly dependent.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>c) Matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are row equivalent, <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n</m>, <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> but <m>B\vec{x} \neq \vec{0}</m>.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>d) <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is an onto linear transform and <m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 4</m>.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>e) <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a one-to-one linear transform, <m>A</m> is <m>6 \times 2</m>, <m>\vec{b}</m> is a vector that is in the co-domain of the transform but not the range of the transform.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <!-- Blank Space -->
  <p><m></m></p><p><m></m></p><p><m></m></p><p><m></m></p><p><m></m></p><p><m></m></p>
  <!-- Question 2 -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (7 points) Indicate <term>true</term> if the statement is true, otherwise, indicate <term>false</term>.
      </p>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <col width="88%" halign="left" />
          <col width="6%" halign="center" />
          <col width="6%" halign="center" />
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>true</cell>
            <cell>false</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>a) The transform <m>T(x_1, x_2, x_3) = (x_1 +1, x_2, x_3)</m> is a linear transform.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>b) If <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 3</m>, then the transformation <m>\vec{x} \to A\vec{x}</m> cannot be one-to-one.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>c) If <m>A</m> is <m>m \times n</m>, <m>\vec{b}</m> is a vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>, and <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is consistent, then <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> pivots.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>d) If an <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> has linearly dependent columns and <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n</m>, then the homogeneous equation <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> must have non-trivial solutions.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>e) If <m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n</m>, and <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^m</m> is not a linear combination of the columns of an <m>m \times n </m> matrix <m>A</m>, then the linear system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> must be inconsistent.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>f) If a matrix <m>A</m> has fewer rows than columns, then the homogeneous system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> must have infinitely many solutions.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>g) If matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are row equivalent, then they have the same RREF.</p></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</page>

<!-- Page 2 -->
<page>
  <!-- Question 3 -->
    <exercise>
      <statement><p>(2 points) Suppose 
      <m>A = 
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \amp 1 \\
        4 \amp 2 
      \end{pmatrix}</m>, 
      <m>\vec{b} = 
      \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \\
        4
      \end{pmatrix}</m>.
      In the grids below, sketch a) the range of the transform <m>x \to Ax</m>, and b) the solution set of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>. You do not need to show your work.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="10% 35% 10% 35% 10%">  
          <p> (a) </p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="se1b-3-a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <p> (b)</p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="pse1b-3-b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
      </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  <!-- Question 4 -->
    <exercise>
      <introduction><p>(7 points) Fill in the blanks. You do not need to justify your reasoning.</p></introduction>
      <task><p>If <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m> and <m>T_A (\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a linear transform that first rotates points in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> counter-clockwise about the origin by <m>\pi/2</m> radians and then reflects them through the line <m>x_1 = 0</m>, then <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 &amp; a_2 \\ a_3 &amp; a_4 \end{pmatrix}</m> where <m>a_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>, <m>a_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>, <m>a_3 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>, and <m>a_4 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>.</p></task>
      <task><p>Suppose matrix <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m>, has exactly one pivot, is in RREF, and every entry of <m>A</m> is either <m>1</m> or <m>0</m>. How many matrices can you construct that meet all these criteria? <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m></p></task>
      <task><p>Suppose <m>T(\vec{x})</m> is a linear transform such that <m>T : \mathbb{R}^c \to \mathbb{R}^d</m>. If <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, and <m>A</m> is <m>12 \times 3</m>, then <m>c = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m> and <m>d = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>.</p></task>
      <task><p>Suppose <m>A</m> is <m>4 \times 6</m>, the linear system <m> A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is consistent, and the parametric vector form of the solution set is <m>\vec{x} = x_2 \vec{v}_1 + x_4 \vec{v}_2</m>. Then <m>\vec{v}_1</m> has <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m> entries, and <m>\vec{b}</m> has <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m> entries.</p></task>
      <task><p>Suppose <m>\vec{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix}
        5 \\
        8 \\
        15
        \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\vec{v}_2 = 
        \begin{pmatrix}
        2 \\
        3 \\
        6
        \end{pmatrix}</m>, and <m>\vec{y} = \begin{pmatrix}
        y_1 \\
        y_2 \\
        y_3
        \end{pmatrix}</m>. If <m>A</m> has <m>2</m> pivots, then transform <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> satisfies <m>T(\vec{v}_1) = 2T(\vec{v}_2) \neq \vec{0}</m>, <m>y_3 = 3</m>, and <m>\vec{y}</m> satisfies <m>A\vec{y} = \vec{0}</m>, then <m>y_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>, <m>y_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>.</p></task>
      <task><p>The linear system whose augmented matrix is <m>
        \left(
          \begin{array}{cc|c}
          1 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\
          2 &amp; k &amp; 1
          \end{array}
        \right)
        </m> is inconsistent when <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>.</p></task>
      <task><p>Consider the linear system <m>x + hy = 3</m> and <m>4x + 8y = k</m>. The system will have no solutions when <m>k = 0</m> and <m>h = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>. But the system will have an infinite number of solutions when <m>h = 2</m> and <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>.</p></task>
      </exercise>
</page>

<!-- Page 3 -->
<page>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (2 points) Suppose <m>\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2</m> are non-zero vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>,
        and that <m>\vec{v}_1</m> and <m>\vec{v}_2</m> span a plane.
        Then <m>S = \text{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2\}</m> is equal to which of the sets below?
        You do not need to show your work.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <col width="60%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="20%" halign="center"/>
        <col width="20%" halign="center"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell/>
          <cell>equal to <m>S</m></cell>
          <cell>not equal to <m>S</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>a) <m>W = \text{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, 3\vec{v}_1\}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>b) <m>U = \text{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_1 + 2\vec{v}_2\}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>c) <m>X = \text{Span}\{\vec{v}_1 + \vec{v}_2, 2\vec{v}_1 + 2\vec{v}_2\}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>d) <m>Y = \text{Span}\{\vec{v}_2, 2\vec{v}_2\}</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\bigcirc</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><p><m></m></p></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        (2 points) Please show your work for this question.
      </p>
      <p>
        For what values of <m>k</m>, if any, are the vectors linearly dependent? Please show your work.
      </p>
      <me>
        v_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ k \end{pmatrix}, \quad
        v_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}, \quad
        v_3 = \begin{pmatrix} -k \\ 6k \\ 8 \end{pmatrix}
      </me>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  </page>


</worksheet>